(1)String Concatenation:-To concatenate, or combine, two strings you can use the + operator.

###Example:-
Merge variable a with variable b into variable c:
Code:-
a = "Hello"
b = "World"
c = a + b
print(c)
Output: "HelloWorld"

#NOTE:- Notice there's no space between "Hello" and "World" because + simply joins the strings exactly as they are.

(2) Adding a space:-To include a space, you can add it explicitly.

Python Code:-
a = "Hello"
b = "World"
c = a + " " + b 
print(c)
Output:- "Hello World"

(3) Use a formatted string (f-string) – more readable:

Python Code:-
a = "Hello"
b = "World"
c = f"{a} {b}"
print(c)
Output:- "Hello World"

(4) Other ways to concatenate

Method	Example	Output
+ operator	"Hello" + "World"	"HelloWorld"
join() method	" ".join(["Hello", "World"])	"Hello World"
f-strings	f"{a} {b}"	"Hello World"
format()	"{} {}".format(a, b)	"Hello World"

#Important note
You can only concatenate strings with +. To combine a string with a number, you must convert the number first:
Code:-
age = 25
message = "I am " + str(age) + " years old"
print(message)
Output:- I am 25 years old
